include includes/folio-shared.pug

html(lang="en")
    head
        +folioHead('Many Hats: one agent was doing every job | Aaron Benjamin', 'A portable AI product team with distinct responsibilities, shared project facts, and human-owned decisions.', '/many-hats.html', 'folio', '/assets/img/folio/project--many-hats/many-hats--work-card-2x.png')
    body.folio.folio--case
        +folioShell
        +folioHeader('work', 'Many Hats', '/#many-hats')
        main#main.folio-main.folio-main--case
            article.folio-case
                header.folio-case__hero
                    .folio-case__hero-media
                        +storyComparison({id: "hats-hero-caption", title: "Many Hats · A product team with explicit boundaries", columns: [{label: "Specialists", title: "Prepare the work", items: ["Direction and domain model", "Flows, language, and interface", "Engineering, evidence, and review"]}, {label: "Independent critic", title: "Challenge the plan", items: ["Inspect the evidence", "Question scope and assumptions", "Record objections"]}, {label: "Human owner", title: "Make the decision", items: ["Choose the tradeoffs", "Approve consequential actions", "Own the outcome"]}], caption: "Roles can recommend and prepare. They cannot turn agreement in a chat into approval."})
                    .folio-case__intro
                        p.folio-eyebrow Open-source agent system
                        h1 Agreement was easy. Accountability needed a structure.
                        p.folio-case__dek I built Many Hats to give AI-assisted product work distinct roles, shared facts, and an independent critic. The human remains accountable for the decision.
                    +caseFacts
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Role
                            dd Author
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Scope
                            dd Thirteen agents and fifteen portable skills
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Key decision
                            dd Separate roles, practice, knowledge, and project work
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Outcome
                            dd Public v0.1 and a replayable, fictional Library Holds demo

                +caseSection({labelledby: "hats-methods-title"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow I — Inspiration
                    h2#hats-methods-title Strategy and product rules arrived as one voice
                    p When one agent frames the opportunity, defines the product, and reviews its own recommendation, a confident answer can conceal several different responsibilities. Many Hats began with the need to make those responsibilities visible and keep the consequential decision with the human.
                    p Design Dash contained 53 stage-oriented methods. They worked in facilitated workshops, but ongoing product work needed clearer responsibility: someone to frame the problem, someone to define the domain, and someone to challenge the plan.
                    p A general agent could draft all three in one thread. Strategy, product rules, and implementation advice arrived as one voice, with no reliable boundary between a recommendation and a decision.
                    p I reviewed public agent collections for role boundaries and verification patterns, then wrote original contracts for the team. Each role has a defined job, evidence expectations, and limits on what it can approve.

                +imageBrief({"role": "Context", "title": "One voice carrying several responsibilities", "composition": "Show strategy, domain modeling, implementation, and review inside one conversation, then identify each responsibility separately.", "purpose": "Establish the opening tension.", "caption": "Conceptual role comparison; no simulated discussion presented as user research.", "ratio": "16:9", "type": "Labeled reconstruction"})
                +caseSection({labelledby: "hats-split-title", tint: true, prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow D — Design Process
                        h2#hats-split-title Stable roles needed a place apart from project work
                        p I separated stable roles from reusable skills, shared product facts, and project deliverables. This makes a correction easier to route. A product-specific rule stays with that project; a reusable practice change needs a motivating case, evaluation, and human approval.
                        p The shared object library belongs in Knowledge, where every role can cite it. The domain specialist stewards it. Projects can reference or fork an object without silently rewriting it for everyone else.
                        p I mapped the 53 Design Dash methods to accountable roles and consolidated them into fifteen portable skills. The original methods remain as references.
                    +storyFlow({id: "hats-planes-caption", title: "Four parts of the system", nodes: [{label: "People", detail: "Stable roles with responsibilities and boundaries."}, {label: "Practice", detail: "Portable skills with testable contracts."}, {label: "Knowledge", detail: "Shared objects, evidence, decisions, and terminology."}, {label: "Work", detail: "Citable artifacts for a specific project."}], caption: "Project corrections stay local. Reusable changes return through a reviewed practice update."})


                +imageBrief({"role": "Process", "title": "The decision and its alternatives", "composition": "Map People, Practice, Knowledge, and Work; show the independent critic and human decision outside implementation ownership.", "purpose": "Make the research-to-decision connection visible.", "caption": "A reconstructed comparison of the choices described in this story.", "ratio": "3:2", "type": "Labeled reconstruction"})
                +caseSection({labelledby: "hats-engagement"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow E — Engagement
                    h2#hats-engagement Let the critic keep its independence
                    p The fictional Library Holds demonstration made the role boundaries inspectable. A product lead framed the direction, a domain specialist defined the objects, and flow and language roles used that model. The critic stayed independent of the plan it was reviewing.
                    p This is a demonstration of collaboration contracts, not a claim that simulated agreement replaces feedback from users or specialists. The human owns the tradeoffs and external actions. Each role prepares evidence and recommendations within a defined responsibility, so a challenge can remain visible even after the direction is accepted.
                +caseSection({labelledby: "hats-demo-title", prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow A — Action
                        h2#hats-demo-title The critic challenged features the MVP didn’t need
                        p Library Holds is a fictional demo of a holds-and-pickup workflow. The tempting scope included branch transfers, waitlist incentives, and a notification preference center. The core problem was simpler: patrons needed to know when a book was ready and when to collect it.
                        p The product lead proposed a small Hold MVP. The domain specialist defined Hold, Patron, Item, and Pickup Window. Flow and content roles worked from that model. The independent critic challenged the extra features; the accepted direction cited that challenge and left them out.
                        p The critic does not inherit responsibility for the plan it reviews. Across the system, agents may prepare changes, but publishing, deployment, and other consequential actions require explicit human authorization.
                    +storyFlow({id: "hats-holds-caption", title: "Library Holds · Fictional demo", nodes: [{label: "Frame", detail: "A reliable holds-and-pickup loop."}, {label: "Model", detail: "Hold, Patron, Item, and Pickup Window."}, {label: "Challenge", detail: "Transfers and preference management exceed the core need."}, {label: "Decide", detail: "Place, cancel, ready, fulfill, and expire. One notice channel."}], caption: "The challenge changes the scope and remains part of the decision record."})

                +caseOutcome("hats-outcome-title", "v0.1 made the approach public and replayable")
                    p v0.1 is public and cloneable. A builder can replay the demo from direction through objects, flow, language, and independent review. Host adapters change how tools discover the skills, not their contracts.
                    p I have no adoption count, and the demo is not a field study. The repository demonstrates the architecture: a specialist leaves a citable artifact, stays within its role, and keeps approval with the human owner.
                +imageBrief({"role": "Outcome", "title": "What the evidence supports", "composition": "Use only the results stated in this case. Keep population, timeframe, qualitative status, and any attribution limits beside the result.", "purpose": "Resolve the story without overstating impact.", "caption": "Evidence summary reconstructed from the documented account; no invented measurements.", "ratio": "16:9", "type": "Evidence summary"})
            +folioFooter(false, 'I’m glad to discuss the boundaries that make AI-assisted product work useful.', {href: '/a-to-z-first-claim.html', label: 'Next: A-to-z First Claim'})
